00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:05.896
Somebody's always trying to do better.

00:16:05.896 --> 00:16:10.390
And if it's at your expense, then you're
just a, you're just a victim.

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:15.391
And, and your jobs are not to be victims.

00:16:15.391 --> 00:16:15.921
It's to have someone else be a victim.

00:16:15.921 --> 00:16:17.466
&gt;&gt; That was very inspiring, Steve.

00:16:17.466 --> 00:16:19.580
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:16:19.580 --> 00:16:22.773
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
At least I've, my takeaway from that was,

00:16:22.773 --> 00:16:27.150
if I walk out and face rejections, I'll
know Steve had faced more.

00:16:27.150 --> 00:16:30.215
[LAUGH].

00:16:30.215 --> 00:16:32.112
&gt;&gt; all, all jokes aside.

00:16:32.112 --> 00:16:36.117
We actually want to now move forward, and
as you mentioned-

00:16:36.117 --> 00:16:38.990
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; The 29 years you've built Blackstone.

00:16:38.990 --> 00:16:42.770
And you've really built it into a
diversified platform with many

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:46.010
distinct business lines and alternative
investments.

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:49.990
So, a lot of people think it's actually
very difficult to be a founder and a CEO.

00:16:51.100 --> 00:16:55.530
And, Mike, because it actually takes
different traits to do both.

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:57.850
But you've clearly succeeded in doing
both.

00:16:57.850 --> 00:17:01.020
So, my question to you, is, do you agree
with that statement?

00:17:01.020 --> 00:17:03.850
And if so, how did you make that
transition?

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:04.891
&gt;&gt; I, I agree completely.

00:17:04.891 --> 00:17:08.638
It, it, it's, these are different kinda
skills.

00:17:08.638 --> 00:17:17.115
Somebody once said to me that no, no one
was born as a CEO.

00:17:17.115 --> 00:17:19.072
That there's not a CEO gene,

00:17:19.072 --> 00:17:23.470
it's a trained behavior not a necessarily
an intuitive one.

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:31.289
And, you know, being an entrepreneur is, a
different set of skills.

00:17:31.289 --> 00:17:35.990
I, I've learned a lot, and I've, I've done
this now for 29 years, so

00:17:35.990 --> 00:17:37.871
there's a lot of evolution.

00:17:37.871 --> 00:17:41.149
I made a, a whole bunch of mistakes
earlier in

00:17:41.149 --> 00:17:46.393
our business's history because I, I
actually didn't really know what to do.

00:17:46.393 --> 00:17:55.383
It's and I got a little coaching from my
partner who had been a CEO twice before.

00:17:55.383 --> 00:18:03.111
But there, there's a, a whole skill set of
learning to be a skee, CEO.

00:18:03.111 --> 00:18:08.753
First, first of all, I mean, I'm sure you
have classes that teach this.

00:18:08.753 --> 00:18:14.196
But, when it's done live it's it's, it's
different.

00:18:14.196 --> 00:18:17.455
One, one of the things that, that, that
is,

00:18:17.455 --> 00:18:22.889
that you have to recognize when you're CEO
is people listen to you way more and

00:18:22.889 --> 00:18:29.080
they, than you'd think and, and they
amplify everything that you say.

00:18:29.080 --> 00:18:33.360
And, and so you have to be exceptionally
careful about what you say.

00:18:34.622 --> 00:18:39.148
ev, even just sort of a joke can get
misinterpreted.

00:18:39.148 --> 00:18:46.502
People look for you know, your, your,
your, your approbation your praise.

00:18:46.502 --> 00:18:50.642
You, you can't give it unless it's
deserved, but, but

00:18:50.642 --> 00:18:55.177
if it's deserved you have to be really
generous doing that because,

00:18:55.177 --> 00:18:58.564
because people respond to it and it's
important.

00:18:59.576 --> 00:19:07.906
You have to realize that human beings are
not like parts of a deal.

00:19:07.906 --> 00:19:12.705
You know, in a deal, it's a, pretty much
of a closed system.

00:19:12.705 --> 00:19:18.374
What one person gets, the other one loses
and that's the way it is.

00:19:18.374 --> 00:19:24.730
With an ongoing team of really terrific
people, you, you can't manage that way.

00:19:24.730 --> 00:19:30.140
You, sometimes you see a problem and you
have to game it out.

00:19:30.140 --> 00:19:34.983
If, if, if people were just like, like
chess pieces, you know,

00:19:34.983 --> 00:19:38.320
you just sort of move em and, and it's
over.

00:19:38.320 --> 00:19:39.090
But they're not.

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:42.790
And, you know, sometimes you can see a
problem and, and

00:19:42.790 --> 00:19:47.740
you have to strategize it out so, so if
you're moving people around,

00:19:47.740 --> 00:19:52.912
you don't want anybody particularly to
lose confidence in themselves.

00:19:52.912 --> 00:19:55.820
You wanna be nice to everybody, but you
want to accomplish the objective.

00:19:55.820 --> 00:19:59.870
So sometimes it can take two years to do
something that you

00:19:59.870 --> 00:20:03.780
know should happen in ten minutes.

00:20:03.780 --> 00:20:07.077
But if you did it, then you'd break so

00:20:07.077 --> 00:20:13.032
much glass through the organization that
you'd threaten the institution or

00:20:13.032 --> 00:20:18.173
you'd threaten core people, and there's a
way to work that out.

00:20:18.173 --> 00:20:23.983
So, so time actually you know, as opposed
to some,

00:20:23.983 --> 00:20:29.690
some cartoon-like swashbuckling CEO.

00:20:30.780 --> 00:20:32.400
It doesn't work that way.

00:20:32.400 --> 00:20:35.600
You, you have to be careful with every
move.

00:20:35.600 --> 00:20:37.758
You have to give people dignity.

00:20:37.758 --> 00:20:39.367
But you have to accomplish your
objectives.

00:20:39.367 --> 00:20:46.078
So time is something that you give up,
sometimes, to do that.

00:20:46.078 --> 00:20:53.570
and, you, you also have to articulate the
core values of your business.

00:20:55.004 --> 00:21:00.610
And you can never stop doing that, and you
can never stop doing that enough.

00:21:00.610 --> 00:21:06.708
Everybody has to know what you stand for,
and they have to see you a lot as

00:21:06.708 --> 00:21:12.470
a CEO, because that way, organizations
don't lose their soul.

00:21:12.470 --> 00:21:17.922
On the other hand, you have to surround
yourself with phenomenal people,

00:21:17.922 --> 00:21:20.096
who share that value system.

00:21:20.096 --> 00:21:25.571
And there's a very interesting almost
internal debate

00:21:25.571 --> 00:21:31.180
you have to go through between control and
ceding control.

00:21:31.180 --> 00:21:36.050
Because nobody wants to work at a place if
they're brilliant,

00:21:36.050 --> 00:21:41.390
terrific people like we have, if they're
so tightly controlled they don't,

00:21:41.390 --> 00:21:45.720
they don't feel they have any autonomy,
or, or, or dignity.

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:50.040
And that may be an issue in finance, not
some other industries.

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:56.530
so, so you have to develop controls, and
the, nobody can blow up the, the firm.

00:21:56.530 --> 00:22:01.570
But autonomy enough so that everybody
thinks that,

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:07.540
that they have self-worth and importance
and can utilize their, their gifts.

00:22:08.940 --> 00:22:14.410
It is very useful as a CEO to have plans
to expand your business,

00:22:14.410 --> 00:22:17.870
not for earnings per share reasons,
interestingly.

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:22.840
It's so, everybody in the organization can
grow up and not be forced out.

00:22:22.840 --> 00:22:27.900
So you start new businesses cuz most
people, in an audience of the type that

00:22:27.900 --> 00:22:34.850
I'm speaking to don't wanna be
subordinated work units.

00:22:34.850 --> 00:22:37.840
That's what they called them in the 1980s.

00:22:37.840 --> 00:22:44.220
You all wanna be at least, brigadier
generals, if, if not more.

00:22:44.220 --> 00:22:46.598
You, you don't want to be like a private
or

00:22:46.598 --> 00:22:49.518
corporal, you want to be in charge of your
destiny.

00:22:49.518 --> 00:22:52.370
You're not so unusual.

00:22:52.370 --> 00:22:58.088
Most people would like to have ownership
of their own growth.

00:22:58.088 --> 00:23:03.189
So, so part of being a CEO is figuring out
how can you provide that for everybody?

00:23:04.230 --> 00:23:08.907
And one way to do that is to start new
businesses where people can move up and,

00:23:08.907 --> 00:23:13.364
and exercise, you know, their creative
abilities, you know, so

00:23:13.364 --> 00:23:17.834
that, you know, a firm is continually
entrepreneurial.

00:23:17.834 --> 00:23:27.140
so, so it, it was a big, step for me to
slow down, cuz I was like a deal person.

00:23:27.140 --> 00:23:29.420
And that's a different world.

00:23:29.420 --> 00:23:32.870
And, and, and just learn how to do that.

00:23:32.870 --> 00:23:37.973
And it's, it's a transition, and you make
mistakes while you're doing that.

00:23:37.973 --> 00:23:40.519
But after you've done it for a long time,
I,

00:23:40.519 --> 00:23:44.465
I actually have fun with, like people who
are newly appointed CEOs.

00:23:44.465 --> 00:23:47.220
So, they'll call me up and say, can I have
lunch with you?

00:23:47.220 --> 00:23:49.750
Can you tell me how to do this job?

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:51.090
And, because they don't know.

00:23:51.090 --> 00:23:56.550
And the smart ones know they don't know
and, and, you know, so none of us know

00:23:56.550 --> 00:24:01.130
everything, but there are, there are like
help, helpful hints as to how to do that.

00:24:02.790 --> 00:24:03.350
&gt;&gt; Thank you, Steve.

00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:08.330
I wanna switch gears a little bit and talk
about your leadership.

00:24:08.330 --> 00:24:11.110
Because View from the Top, as you know, is
a series of leadership.

00:24:12.846 --> 00:24:18.254
We wanna know, what was your personal
leadership philosophy

00:24:18.254 --> 00:24:23.669
that led you to lead bl, Blackstone over
the past 29 years?

00:24:23.669 --> 00:24:26.730
You know, I, I've have always been a
leader.

00:24:27.840 --> 00:24:29.140
You know, I was like one of those-

00:24:29.140 --> 00:24:29.700
&gt;&gt; Apologies.

00:24:29.700 --> 00:24:33.529
&gt;&gt; Student council, choose your high
school people and,

00:24:33.529 --> 00:24:38.580
you know, you know, president of your high
school people, and

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:43.884
did stuff in college like that, did stuff
in business school like that, so.

00:24:43.884 --> 00:24:46.616
I don't, I don't know how I got that way.

00:24:46.616 --> 00:24:49.390
I, I've always been like that.

00:24:49.390 --> 00:24:53.851
I've always enjoyed being in charge of
things and doing new things.

00:24:53.851 --> 00:24:58.763
And, and I remember I brought a rock group
that was, that was famous at the time to

00:24:58.763 --> 00:25:02.780
my high school, and everybody said it
couldn't be done.

00:25:02.780 --> 00:25:06.191
I thought it would be fun, and, you know,
sort of interesting.

00:25:06.191 --> 00:25:10.191
So, I like doing new things, and I like
being a leader, and

00:25:10.191 --> 00:25:16.390
I like dealing with, complexity, and I, I
like dealing with problems.

00:25:16.390 --> 00:25:21.310
I like creating, and, and usually that's
the best spot to be in.

00:25:24.430 --> 00:25:28.660
&gt;&gt; So, here we have a course at the GSV,
it's called Touchy Feely.

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:32.850
It many of you might have taken it here.

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:36.660
Basically, it's about how to be a leader
by being more aware of

00:25:36.660 --> 00:25:38.540
your emotional reactions.

00:25:38.540 --> 00:25:43.537
And building deeper connection with people
through self disclosure,

00:25:43.537 --> 00:25:48.044
vulnerability, and really showing your
authentic emotions.

00:25:48.044 --> 00:25:51.237
So, we're actually trying to figure that
out is,

00:25:51.237 --> 00:25:55.928
how do we apply that in our leadership
style and especially in a work place?

00:25:55.928 --> 00:25:59.180
So what's your take on this touchy feely
kind of leadership style?

00:26:01.140 --> 00:26:04.642
&gt;&gt; well, I I, I went to a different
business school.

00:26:04.642 --> 00:26:10.715
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:26:10.715 --> 00:26:13.395
&gt;&gt; This, this course potentially, at least
when I went,

00:26:13.395 --> 00:26:16.599
could have been antithetical to everything
that people believe.

00:26:17.634 --> 00:26:21.380
And [LAUGH], and it was, it was pretty
rough.

00:26:22.502 --> 00:26:26.066
But I understand where you're coming from.

00:26:26.066 --> 00:26:28.648
I, I, I think it's-

00:26:28.648 --> 00:26:30.636
&gt;&gt; Thanks for understanding.

00:26:30.636 --> 00:26:33.520
&gt;&gt; Okay.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:26:33.520 --> 00:26:35.780
&gt;&gt; Do, do we have a therapist in the group
here?

00:26:35.780 --> 00:26:36.950
Say, step up.

00:26:38.280 --> 00:26:43.320
The I, I, I think it's important as, as I
was saying before to

00:26:43.320 --> 00:26:48.870
treat people in a way where they feel
secure, or where they feel valued.

00:26:49.980 --> 00:26:54.190
Where they, they feel that you must tell
the truth.

00:26:55.468 --> 00:27:00.870
And there are different ways of, of doing
that.

00:27:00.870 --> 00:27:07.420
But in our investment process, for
example, we, we, I was gonna say something

00:27:07.420 --> 00:27:11.230
like, we encourage that people come
forward with different points of view.

00:27:11.230 --> 00:27:12.751
In fact, we demand it.

00:27:12.751 --> 00:27:14.077
It's not encouraging.

00:27:14.077 --> 00:27:16.636
And, and that's because I did a,

00:27:16.636 --> 00:27:21.590
I approved a terrible investment as the
second investment we did.

00:27:21.590 --> 00:27:27.150
And we lost money, and it was incredibly
devastating to me.

00:27:27.150 --> 00:27:30.790
because, as long as you're talking about
emotions, I,

00:27:30.790 --> 00:27:33.360
I had some investor who wanted to see us,
and he started screaming at me.

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:36.640
I'd never been screamed at before and

00:27:36.640 --> 00:27:39.620
nobody in my house had ever raised their
voice.

00:27:39.620 --> 00:27:43.410
And here's this person screaming for
about, felt like an hour.

00:27:43.410 --> 00:27:44.980
It was five minutes.

00:27:44.980 --> 00:27:47.100
Completely outta control.

00:27:47.100 --> 00:27:54.870
And I mean, I, I was I, I almost wanted to
cry, actually because he was right.

00:27:54.870 --> 00:27:58.899
I, I had made the wrong decision.

00:27:58.899 --> 00:28:02.252
And that's because we didn't have as
organized an investment process cuz

00:28:02.252 --> 00:28:02.845
we were new.

00:28:02.845 --> 00:28:09.124
So, so the best thing we did,
retrospectively, although it was gruesome,

00:28:09.124 --> 00:28:15.403
was, was to lose that money because we set
up a process where everybody, in,

00:28:15.403 --> 00:28:22.048
at the firm at that time, was just private
equity, now we're doing a lot more things.

00:28:22.048 --> 00:28:26.620
We had to go around the table and everyone
had to express themselves.

00:28:26.620 --> 00:28:33.040
And they had to do it with something
negative about the proposed investment,

00:28:33.040 --> 00:28:35.718
risks that people didn't see.

00:28:35.718 --> 00:28:40.128
And so we got used to if you brought an
investment, you knew you,

00:28:40.128 --> 00:28:43.260
you weren't gonna get universal
approbation.

00:28:44.310 --> 00:28:49.690
The job of everyone around the table was
to critique it so that we, we,

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:52.840
we got rid of the risk or understood it.

00:28:52.840 --> 00:28:55.348
&gt;&gt; At the GSB we call that giving
feedback.

00:28:55.348 --> 00:29:00.407
&gt;&gt; Yeah but, it's, it's, it's giving
feedback that gets people angry,

00:29:00.407 --> 00:29:02.351
that's the problem right?

00:29:02.351 --> 00:29:03.129
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:29:03.129 --> 00:29:11.069
&gt;&gt; So, so, so you, you have to set up an
environment where people feel secure that,

00:29:11.069 --> 00:29:17.980
that, that act of criticism is not
personal, it's impersonal.

00:29:17.980 --> 00:29:21.020
It has nothing to do with you bringing the
deal.

00:29:21.020 --> 00:29:25.390
It has nothing to do with them pointing
out the shortfalls in the deal,

00:29:25.390 --> 00:29:28.370
because every deal will be treated exactly
the same, and

00:29:28.370 --> 00:29:30.760
every person will have that same role.

00:29:30.760 --> 00:29:34.674
So what we tried to do as, as part of an
overall I

00:29:34.674 --> 00:29:40.046
don't know whether it's touchy feely or,
or you know, it's,

00:29:40.046 --> 00:29:45.055
it's more in, institutional comfort for
individuals is to

00:29:45.055 --> 00:29:51.366
have that direct conversation in a way
that ma, made it, made it non-personal.

00:29:51.366 --> 00:29:56.340
And, so, so the concept of worrying about
these things which is, I think,

00:29:56.340 --> 00:30:02.895
what you're actually asking, is, is, is,
is really important to have an ongoing.

00:30:02.895 --> 00:30:09.590
Successful business, but to have the
people in it always feel protected.

00:30:10.930 --> 00:30:17.070
and, and we set up processes like that
and, and, you know, we have we have very,

00:30:17.070 --> 00:30:21.905
very, very low turnover at, at our firm.

00:30:21.905 --> 00:30:23.340
And I guess the last two or

00:30:23.340 --> 00:30:27.800
three years we were voted like the best
place to work in finance.

00:30:29.206 --> 00:30:33.930
By Pension and Investment Age and some
other stuff, and so forth.

00:30:33.930 --> 00:30:38.470
So, care to these issues is, is really
important.

00:30:40.660 --> 00:30:45.350
&gt;&gt; You mentioned not, not keeping those
criticism personal.

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:47.890
But I actually wanna get a little bit more
personal with you.

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:51.500
Which is, I wanna learn more about your
personal background and motivations.

00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:56.400
So when you were first graduating from
HBS, what motivated you back then?

00:30:56.400 --> 00:30:57.930
And now, now what motivates you now?

00:30:57.930 --> 00:31:00.509
And how has that changed over the years?

00:31:00.509 --> 00:31:01.948
&gt;&gt; Well, there are a few answers to that.

00:31:01.948 --> 00:31:10.266
When I, I remember interviewing at,
[LAUGH] it's a shame this is video.

00:31:10.266 --> 00:31:12.393
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:31:12.393 --> 00:31:18.139
&gt;&gt; That, that, when I was graduating from
college, I was interviewing with,

00:31:18.139 --> 00:31:22.650
with some employer, and they said, well,
what did you want to be?

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:28.030
Why do you want to work here?

00:31:28.030 --> 00:31:32.565
I, and I said, well I, I, I wanna be a
telephone switchboard.

00:31:34.140 --> 00:31:35.090
And the person looked at me.

00:31:35.090 --> 00:31:36.970
He said, what are you talking about?

00:31:36.970 --> 00:31:41.750
I said, I wanna have all these inputs
running up my arm,

00:31:41.750 --> 00:31:45.350
going in my head, twist them around, and
shoot them out some other place.

00:31:45.350 --> 00:31:46.270
That's what I want to do.

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:48.600
As a process.

00:31:49.600 --> 00:31:53.280
And the guy looked at me and said, you're
wrong for us.

00:31:53.280 --> 00:31:55.920
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:31:55.920 --> 00:31:58.590
&gt;&gt; but, but what, what.

00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:02.410
By the time I was a little more you know,
sorta developed.

00:32:02.410 --> 00:32:04.650
I graduated from business school.

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:06.630
I, I still wanted to do the same thing.

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:10.368
It's this insatiable need to learn what's
going on and

00:32:10.368 --> 00:32:16.500
see something new, and, and do something
as a result of that.

00:32:16.500 --> 00:32:20.120
And that's, that's never really stopped
now.

00:32:20.120 --> 00:32:23.060
That, that expresses itself in, in
different ways.

00:32:23.060 --> 00:32:24.570
I also wanted to make a lot of money.

00:32:24.570 --> 00:32:28.520
I guess you're not allowed to say that
anymore because that's unfashionable.

00:32:28.520 --> 00:32:32.140
Or if you only have to do it through
capital gains with new apps.

00:32:32.140 --> 00:32:34.090
But, but, back,

00:32:34.090 --> 00:32:39.590
back in the olden days you, you sorta did
it, you know, with more current income.

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:44.560
And so I, I, I had, I had $1400 when I
graduated.

00:32:46.650 --> 00:32:52.330
from, from money that somebody, group of
people, gave me on my bar mitzvah.

00:32:52.330 --> 00:32:56.540
I didn't even know my parents had held it
aside, and I, I was getting married,

00:32:56.540 --> 00:33:01.900
so I saw this wonderful ring at Tiffany's
for 1300.

00:33:01.900 --> 00:33:04.230
So I sorta looked at it.

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:05.750
I said don't you have something a little
better?

00:33:05.750 --> 00:33:07.080
I might as well blow the whole thing.

00:33:07.080 --> 00:33:09.378
So I spent 1400 I started at zero.

00:33:09.378 --> 00:33:16.020
[LAUGH] So, I, I decided I, I, I needed
fuel.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:18.180
So that was one of my objectives.

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:24.950
But the, the basic objective was, was to
put yourself in situations.

00:33:24.950 --> 00:33:27.580
Where the, the learning curve was so

00:33:27.580 --> 00:33:33.050
steep, and, and the outcomes were so
consequential.

00:33:33.050 --> 00:33:37.450
That it just was gonna be f, really
fascinating and

00:33:37.450 --> 00:33:43.460
interesting and fun, and I'm still doing
that, and I love it.

00:33:43.460 --> 00:33:47.510
It's great.
I, it's yesterday I

00:33:47.510 --> 00:33:51.290
had Henry Kissinger come over he was doing
a video for

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:55.460
the Schwatzman Scholars and it's nice
venue to do that.

00:33:55.460 --> 00:33:58.298
He's, he's on our advisory board and

00:33:58.298 --> 00:34:02.372
we were walking down the hall after he'd
done this video.

00:34:02.372 --> 00:34:06.957
And, and you know, he said something like,
gee, Steve, you know, like, 91 and

00:34:06.957 --> 00:34:09.269
a half, I still feel like I'm in my 30s.

00:34:09.269 --> 00:34:14.260
And, and, he said, he said, I think you're
the same.

00:34:14.260 --> 00:34:18.320
I said, yeah, except I'm not 91 and a
half, but, but, but, but I,

00:34:18.320 --> 00:34:21.060
you know, feel exactly the same way.

00:34:21.060 --> 00:34:23.355
And he said, you, you'd never retire,
would you?

00:34:23.355 --> 00:34:26.576
And I, I said well, why would I retire
when my life's so

00:34:26.576 --> 00:34:29.330
interesting, and I'm learning all the
time.

00:34:29.330 --> 00:34:34.070
And I'm traveling all over the world and
it's great, it's fantastic.

00:34:34.070 --> 00:34:41.610
And he said yes, so he said I do what,
what I do, f, for, for no compensation.

00:34:41.610 --> 00:34:44.813
And I said, yeah, I, I get it, I mean
absolutely.

00:34:44.813 --> 00:34:48.520
I mean it's as you'll find cause they're
mostly young people in the audience.

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:54.670
That, when you find something you love
and, and you're good at it and,

00:34:54.670 --> 00:34:59.670
you know, you go to your touchy feely
course and your self-actualized.

00:34:59.670 --> 00:35:01.690
Right?
I mean that's, that's what it's like.

00:35:01.690 --> 00:35:02.630
That's the game.

00:35:02.630 --> 00:35:04.680
It's, it's no, it's not about money.

00:35:04.680 --> 00:35:06.160
It's about creation.

00:35:06.160 --> 00:35:11.420
It, it's about feeling you know, you're
totally alive everyday and

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:12.370
it's interesting.

00:35:12.370 --> 00:35:16.090
And you run into heads of state or,

00:35:16.090 --> 00:35:22.240
or finance ministers and my kinda industry
or with unique transaction.

00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:26.830
So doing things that are the biggest in
the world with great returns and

00:35:26.830 --> 00:35:27.900
doing unusual things.

00:35:27.900 --> 00:35:30.300
You're always out on our front tier.

00:35:30.300 --> 00:35:37.320
This type of life is completely seductive
and, and it's, it's not age determinant.

00:35:38.450 --> 00:35:39.638
It's really.

00:35:39.638 --> 00:35:45.180
it's, it's really about you.

00:35:45.180 --> 00:35:52.580
And remember when I was interviewing for a
job just graduating from business school.

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:57.230
And I went to some firm and you meet a lot
of people when you're interviewing.

00:35:57.230 --> 00:35:58.570
It's very interesting.

00:35:58.570 --> 00:36:01.660
And I was sitting in front of a guy who
looked like he was in his 80s,

00:36:02.740 --> 00:36:05.250
named, his name was Clifford Michelle.

00:36:06.260 --> 00:36:10.680
And he had all these bound volumes of
financings and things he'd done.

00:36:10.680 --> 00:36:15.310
And the guy had these brilliant blue eyes,
and he was sitting alertly,

00:36:15.310 --> 00:36:20.520
and he's in his 80s, and the guy was
amazing.

00:36:21.650 --> 00:36:22.670
And I said, you know what?

00:36:23.830 --> 00:36:29.270
I wanna be him.
I wanna be somebody at that age

00:36:29.270 --> 00:36:35.090
can do what he's doing cuz he's completely
engaged, with life.

00:36:35.090 --> 00:36:39.190
You can do that, and that's the fun of it,
and that's what I wanted to do, and.

00:36:39.190 --> 00:36:43.850
You know I was lucky, to be in an industry
that

00:36:43.850 --> 00:36:49.780
was going through enormous expansion at
the time when I was a young person.

00:36:49.780 --> 00:36:53.880
And so I was lucky, and I didn't blow
myself up, that was good too.

00:36:54.990 --> 00:36:57.380
And, and, I have one saying in.

00:36:58.390 --> 00:36:59.870
To those of you who are interested in
finances,

00:36:59.870 --> 00:37:02.882
which is apparently like 12 people in your
whole school now.

00:37:02.882 --> 00:37:08.950
[LAUGH] You know that, that there are,

00:37:08.950 --> 00:37:12.440
there are no brave old people in finance.

00:37:13.650 --> 00:37:16.104
They usually get blown up by the time
they're 40.

00:37:17.210 --> 00:37:20.940
And if they're given authority by the time
they're 45.

00:37:20.940 --> 00:37:23.640
Because you always have to have balanced
risk,

00:37:23.640 --> 00:37:27.866
you always have to worry about your down
side.

00:37:27.866 --> 00:37:31.194
If I, if I could say just one thing, as a
little commercial for

00:37:31.194 --> 00:37:33.695
the Schwartzman Scholars program.

00:37:33.695 --> 00:37:36.411
This is like really a wonderful thing
we're doing.

00:37:36.411 --> 00:37:37.938
It's like the Rhoades.

00:37:37.938 --> 00:37:40.780
except, you get to go to Tsinghua
University,

00:37:40.780 --> 00:37:43.643
where we have a distinguished graduate.

00:37:43.643 --> 00:37:46.139
and, and, and,

00:37:46.139 --> 00:37:52.810
it's gonna be a marvelous introduction to
China for one year, program.

00:37:52.810 --> 00:37:54.850
where, you know, be taught in English.

00:37:56.570 --> 00:37:58.570
It's the universal language.

00:37:58.570 --> 00:38:01.472
And, you know, we have three different
majors.

00:38:01.472 --> 00:38:06.298
But we're going to start out with core
courses everything about China and

00:38:06.298 --> 00:38:08.860
have a leadership course.

00:38:08.860 --> 00:38:11.430
Designed by the other business school.

00:38:11.430 --> 00:38:14.310
Where they have cold weather.

00:38:14.310 --> 00:38:15.520
On the other side, of the country, and
then, you,

00:38:15.520 --> 00:38:17.690
you'll have courses in your major.

00:38:17.690 --> 00:38:20.630
Everybody will get a mentor, from the real
world.

00:38:20.630 --> 00:38:22.730
Some of the people, I haven't disclosed
yet.

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:29.880
I mean, they're some of the most famous
people in the world.

00:38:29.880 --> 00:38:35.730
and, ya, you, you get to take trips,
around China.

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:38.750
You'll meet the leaders of the country.

00:38:38.750 --> 00:38:44.104
And, and then we'll have a study flow of
really fascinating people that

00:38:44.104 --> 00:38:49.041
come to Beijing doing this in a, a new
building that we built.

00:38:49.041 --> 00:38:51.335
That looks like a college at Oxford or

00:38:51.335 --> 00:38:54.961
Cambridge, except it's looks like a
Chinese building.

00:38:54.961 --> 00:38:58.422
Same thing, residential college and

00:38:58.422 --> 00:39:03.240
we're having some meeting, Rob, I think,
later today?

00:39:03.240 --> 00:39:06.931
Yeah, we are, I guess people are allowed
to come?

00:39:06.931 --> 00:39:09.416
So where is this mystery meeting?

00:39:09.416 --> 00:39:09.916
&gt;&gt; Wallenberg Hall.

00:39:12.910 --> 00:39:16.230
Okay, so I got my commercial in, which I
wasn't supposed to do,

00:39:16.230 --> 00:39:18.235
but then again, I am an entrepreneur.

00:39:18.235 --> 00:39:20.900
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Thank you Steve.

00:39:20.900 --> 00:39:23.590
Thank you for answering all the questions
with such tremendous candor,

00:39:23.590 --> 00:39:24.340
I really appreciate it.

00:39:24.340 --> 00:39:27.700
At this point, I am going to have everyone
here in

00:39:27.700 --> 00:39:31.230
the audience have an opportunity to ask
Steve any questions you want.

00:39:31.230 --> 00:39:34.210
There are two mics going around the
auditorium.

00:39:34.210 --> 00:39:36.430
Ryan on the left, Caroline on the right.

00:39:36.430 --> 00:39:40.840
We're also taking questions from Twitter,
especially if you're sitting up there.

00:39:40.840 --> 00:39:45.640
We don't have mics there, so please Tweet
you questions to, GSB VFTT.

00:39:45.640 --> 00:39:49.030
I'll start with a question from Twitter.

00:39:50.670 --> 00:39:51.210
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:53.180
So this is a question that came into
Twitter.

00:39:54.210 --> 00:39:57.980
As you grew Blackstone, how did you ensure
you got the right people for

00:39:57.980 --> 00:39:58.480
the right seats?

00:39:59.510 --> 00:40:00.450
&gt;&gt; To the right people?

00:40:00.450 --> 00:40:02.800
&gt;&gt; For the right seats at Blackstone.

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:07.300
&gt;&gt; How did, hiring is a wonderful
exercise.

00:40:08.540 --> 00:40:09.937
It's really fun.

00:40:09.937 --> 00:40:15.580
What you're trying to do is,

00:40:15.580 --> 00:40:19.840
is match people's capability with, with
who they really are.

00:40:19.840 --> 00:40:23.340
One thing we're doing with for the
Schwarzman Scholars program is trying to,

00:40:23.340 --> 00:40:25.740
to figure out who will be great future
leaders.

00:40:25.740 --> 00:40:29.260
And, and then give them a year to learn
about China.

00:40:29.260 --> 00:40:32.360
And, and people say, you know, ha, ha, how
to you know who's gonna be good.

00:40:33.460 --> 00:40:36.840
And you, you have to talk to them.

00:40:36.840 --> 00:40:38.120
It, it's not a resume.

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:41.660
A resume, it’s like a building block.

00:40:41.660 --> 00:40:42.910
Are they smart, or are they dumb?

00:40:42.910 --> 00:40:45.480
If, if they're dumb you don't see them.

00:40:45.480 --> 00:40:50.300
but, but smart people come in much
different versions.

00:40:50.300 --> 00:40:56.800
And, and you know, when, when, when people
like myself meet other people I,

00:40:56.800 --> 00:40:59.930
I, I don't know how to interview anybody.

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:05.420
They, they walk in to your room and they
sit down and you look at them.

00:41:06.550 --> 00:41:09.380
And somebody says something.

00:41:09.380 --> 00:41:11.740
Or they have something on their resume
that's interesting.

00:41:11.740 --> 00:41:16.190
This is, you know, like the, somebody
who's won the World Chess Championship and

00:41:16.190 --> 00:41:20.140
still runs the 400 meters in less than
four minutes.

00:41:20.140 --> 00:41:23.600
And you go, when do you spend time to do
all this stuff?

00:41:23.600 --> 00:41:25.030
And you start a conversation.

00:41:25.030 --> 00:41:31.040
And what you're trying to do is figure out
how flexible their minds

00:41:31.040 --> 00:41:34.180
are how emotionally stable they are.

00:41:34.180 --> 00:41:36.153
How they will do under stress?

00:41:36.153 --> 00:41:43.020
Are they self-reliant what,

00:41:43.020 --> 00:41:47.640
what's their ability to express
themselves?

00:41:47.640 --> 00:41:52.540
Sometimes you can see fear in people's
eyes.

00:41:52.540 --> 00:41:54.900
This is usually not a good thing, right?

00:41:54.900 --> 00:41:56.960
Fear.
I mean like complete fear.

00:41:56.960 --> 00:41:58.410
What are they doing sitting there?

00:41:58.410 --> 00:41:59.410
I, I'm sorta easy.

00:42:00.420 --> 00:42:01.630
They shouldn't be scared of me.

00:42:01.630 --> 00:42:04.860
If they're scared of me, they're gonna be
scared of somebody else,

00:42:04.860 --> 00:42:08.860
and, and that's not good, at least from a
sales perspectives.

00:42:08.860 --> 00:42:10.150
You know, being scared.

00:42:10.150 --> 00:42:13.410
So there are all kinds of things that
people will tell you.

00:42:13.410 --> 00:42:19.570
And you're trying to burrow into their
head and predict future behavior.

00:42:19.570 --> 00:42:22.390
And, and if you can do that and, and

00:42:22.390 --> 00:42:24.440
you have certain criteria that they have
to be nice.

00:42:26.210 --> 00:42:30.860
We don't, we don't hire anybody that's not
nice, or anybody that's political.

00:42:30.860 --> 00:42:33.120
That's what happens, cuz they're a bunch
of,

00:42:33.120 --> 00:42:35.750
there's entrepreneur stuff in the air
here.

00:42:35.750 --> 00:42:38.770
So you don't have to hire anybody who's
disagreeable,

00:42:39.770 --> 00:42:41.290
just because they wanna be hired.

00:42:41.290 --> 00:42:42.900
You don't have to do that.

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:48.020
So you can make whatever culture you want
to make.

00:42:48.020 --> 00:42:50.680
It's hard at the beginning cuz not
everybody wants to join you

00:42:50.680 --> 00:42:53.370
because you're not successful so you're
desperate.

00:42:53.370 --> 00:42:56.780
Right?
But you'll find that you don't do well

00:42:56.780 --> 00:43:01.790
unless you're hiring people who are
consistent with your values.

00:43:01.790 --> 00:43:05.497
So you've got to know yourself and you
gotta stick to those kinda people.

00:43:07.210 --> 00:43:08.120
Meritocracy.

00:43:09.140 --> 00:43:10.360
Good thing.

00:43:10.360 --> 00:43:15.536
You know, sorta being able to operate
horizontally instead of being, you know,

00:43:15.536 --> 00:43:18.170
sorta a controlled monster.

00:43:18.170 --> 00:43:19.540
We, we can't do those.

00:43:19.540 --> 00:43:21.480
You can't have those people around.

00:43:21.480 --> 00:43:22.530
Even if they're smart.

00:43:23.560 --> 00:43:27.720
Even if I see movies with people who are
like that and smart.

00:43:27.720 --> 00:43:32.500
It's, it doesnt work well as you get more
mature.

00:43:32.500 --> 00:43:33.809
You can have exceptions.

00:43:33.809 --> 00:43:40.217
But basically you, you, you have to have
people who you think can really adapt,

00:43:40.217 --> 00:43:45.431
really have a gift, really are nice,
really can communicate.

00:43:45.431 --> 00:43:49.420
And you have to imagine them under big
stress.

00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:51.520
How will they behave?

00:43:51.520 --> 00:43:52.210
Will they be honest?

00:43:52.210 --> 00:43:53.080
Things like that.

00:43:53.080 --> 00:43:57.160
&gt;&gt; Thank you.
Do we have more questions?

00:43:57.160 --> 00:44:00.850
Right there.

00:44:02.190 --> 00:44:03.420
Thank you.

00:44:03.420 --> 00:44:06.815
&gt;&gt; So, when you were first starting your
business, all, all entrepreneurs have to,

00:44:06.815 --> 00:44:09.850
sorta overcome an established order.

00:44:09.850 --> 00:44:13.600
In your case, you mentioned that you had a
great team wearing a brand new jersey.

00:44:13.600 --> 00:44:17.402
And you found it hard to get people to, to
deal with you, so to speak.

00:44:17.402 --> 00:44:20.190
What motivates that sorta conservativism.

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:21.660
How did you overcome that?

00:44:21.660 --> 00:44:25.260
And, and how did you sort of build
Blackstone into today's establishment?

00:44:29.160 --> 00:44:35.237
&gt;&gt; Finishing the last answer, one of the
thing as you're hiring people that

00:44:35.237 --> 00:44:40.969
people over 40 are their reputation.

00:44:44.000 --> 00:44:47.630
Below that, they change, they adapt.

00:44:47.630 --> 00:44:51.915
When they're over 40, if you're
interviewing somebody, and

00:44:51.915 --> 00:44:54.065
you think you're a good person.

00:44:54.065 --> 00:44:59.765
But like five people tell you they're
really not, don't believe yourself.

00:44:59.765 --> 00:45:01.449
Believe the five people.

00:45:01.449 --> 00:45:03.851
Who've had experiences with them.

00:45:03.851 --> 00:45:06.436
Don't let your own perception,

00:45:06.436 --> 00:45:12.540
somebody who's really a certain
personality overrule the other stuff.

00:45:12.540 --> 00:45:15.240
Every time I did it,I was wrong.

00:45:15.240 --> 00:45:17.450
So I've learned some lessons.

00:45:17.450 --> 00:45:23.961
The question about the world not accepting
new things and how do you change it?

00:45:23.961 --> 00:45:31.057
I've, I've, I've learned that, other than
at Stanford and, you know,

00:45:31.057 --> 00:45:37.519
this part of California, people basically
don't like change.

00:45:38.850 --> 00:45:43.830
As distressing as it is to imagine that,
they don't like change.

00:45:43.830 --> 00:45:46.470
Every time we've tried to change
something, for

00:45:46.470 --> 00:45:48.910
example, we renovate, we were renovating
a,

00:45:48.910 --> 00:45:55.830
a hotel in London that was called
Claridge's, and it was very prominent.

00:45:55.830 --> 00:45:59.540
And we bought in 1995 and it was quite
run-down and

00:45:59.540 --> 00:46:05.770
needed to be you know, sorta modernized in
a variety of ways.

00:46:05.770 --> 00:46:10.580
And I got these endless barrage of things.

00:46:10.580 --> 00:46:13.480
Don't change it, right?

00:46:13.480 --> 00:46:15.940
Don't change it, it's so wonderful.

00:46:15.940 --> 00:46:19.490
Don't change a stick of furniture even
though you

00:46:20.690 --> 00:46:26.490
were sitting in a couch and your knees
were over your eyes, right?

00:46:26.490 --> 00:46:28.140
Don't change anything.

00:46:28.140 --> 00:46:31.130
And, you know, you get sort of like
protests and

00:46:31.130 --> 00:46:32.620
so forth, so, so we change things.

00:46:32.620 --> 00:46:35.300
But, but we, we didn't change it bad.

00:46:35.300 --> 00:46:37.230
We changed them good.

00:46:37.230 --> 00:46:41.590
And I had one particular person who was
born there during World War II.

00:46:41.590 --> 00:46:46.848
Who you know, really became very unhappy.

00:46:46.848 --> 00:46:50.960
And so, so you know, we did the change
with English heritage.

00:46:50.960 --> 00:46:53.270
Everything was like beautifully done.

00:46:53.270 --> 00:46:59.200
And at the end of that he wrote me this
long letter saying

00:47:01.520 --> 00:47:05.260
how wrong he was and, and how obnoxious he
was.

00:47:05.260 --> 00:47:07.800
And this is a famous person.

00:47:07.800 --> 00:47:14.740
And it's absolutely wonderful and I just
wanted to tell you I was completely wrong.

00:47:14.740 --> 00:47:16.630
You usually don't get that satisfaction.

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:21.940
but, but change is really hard for a lot
of people.

00:47:21.940 --> 00:47:26.070
And, and so you have to recognize that
whenever you start something new and

00:47:26.070 --> 00:47:28.940
you go out of your way to make them
comfortable.

00:47:30.490 --> 00:47:34.450
Because you can introduce them to change.

00:47:34.450 --> 00:47:38.410
At, at, at Blackstone it's not directly
applicable.

00:47:38.410 --> 00:47:40.260
We keep doing new things.

00:47:40.260 --> 00:47:45.730
And as long as you, you really do good
returns and people know

00:47:45.730 --> 00:47:50.020
that you care about them, the investors,
and you'll never do anything to hurt them.

00:47:50.020 --> 00:47:52.810
And you're on their team, on their side.

00:47:52.810 --> 00:47:57.180
And you're, you're just, like trying to
make their lives better.

00:47:57.180 --> 00:47:59.460
They have to understand your motives.

00:48:00.620 --> 00:48:04.170
And if you can explain your motives, and
your motives are direct and

00:48:04.170 --> 00:48:08.560
pure, really, I mean, we wanna make money,
but we, we only wanna do this for

00:48:08.560 --> 00:48:12.200
the benefit of people we're investing for.

00:48:12.200 --> 00:48:15.820
And if they never un, if they understand
that you never

00:48:15.820 --> 00:48:22.630
will introduce a product that, that is not
terrific.

00:48:22.630 --> 00:48:25.810
You believe completely in it, that's your
business.

